AUTO-IMPLEMENENTED PROPERTY ->
An auto-implemented property is a property in which the C# compiler 
automatically creates a hidden backing field, so the programmer does not need to explicitly declare one.
Example -> public string FirstName { get; set; }

COMPUTED PROPERTY ->
A computed property is a property whose value is calculated 
dynamically from other fields or properties each time it is accessed, rather than being stored in memory.
Example ->  public double Area
          {
              get
              {
                  return Math.PI * Radius * Radius;
              }
          }




✅ Composition (♦)

Ask:

Can the child exist without the parent? → No
Is the child owned exclusively by one parent? → Yes
If the parent is deleted, should the child also be deleted? → Yes

Example: Patient ♦── MedicalRecord

✅ Aggregation (◇)

Ask:

Can the child exist without the parent? → Yes
Can the child be shared or moved to another parent? → Yes
If the parent is deleted, does the child still exist? → Yes

Example: Department ◇── Doctor